Super Metroid, recompiled to run natively on Windows, macOS, and Linux, with
a sharper picture, cleaner sound, a settings menu, and optional cheats.


STARTING THE GAME

macOS    Open the disk image and drag SuperMetroidX to Applications. The app
         is not notarized, so macOS asks before it opens the first time:
         open it, then choose Open Anyway in System Settings > Privacy &
         Security.
Windows  Unzip the folder and run SuperMetroidX.exe. If SmartScreen warns
         about an unrecognized app, choose More info, then Run anyway.
Linux    Unpack the archive and run bin/SuperMetroidX. To add the game to
         your applications menu, copy the bin and share folders into
         ~/.local.

A build without the ROM built in looks for SuperMetroid.sfc in the save
folder (below), or takes a ROM path on the command line.


CONTROLS

SNES         Keyboard       Xbox-style gamepad
D-pad        Arrow keys     D-pad or left stick
X  (shoot)   S              Y
A  (jump)    A              B
B  (run)     Z              A
Y            X              X
Select       Right Shift    Back / View
L / R        Q / W          Shoulders or triggers
Start        Return         Start / Menu

~ (R3 on a gamepad)   Settings menu: display, sound, and cheats
F11 or Alt+Return     Fullscreen or window
F5 / F9               Quick-save / quick-load
1 to 5                Cheats: life, ammo, items, hyper beam, fly
Escape                Quit


SAVES AND SETTINGS

macOS    ~/Library/Application Support/SuperMetroidX/SuperMetroidX/
Windows  %APPDATA%\SuperMetroidX\SuperMetroidX\
Linux    ~/.local/share/SuperMetroidX/SuperMetroidX/

The game saves at save stations, as on a console, to SuperMetroid.srm.
Settings from the menu are kept in settings.ini. If the window ever ends up
unusable, start the game with --reset-settings, or delete settings.ini.
SuperMetroidX --help lists every command-line option.


